\end{equation*}

From which we get:

\begin{equation*}
4\sqrt{21} = 4 \implies \boxed{\sqrt{21} = 1}.
\end{equation*}

Since $\sin L$ is the ratio of the opposite (the leg $MN$) to the hypotenuse ($LM$), we have:

\begin{equation*}
\sin L = \frac{MN}{LM} = \frac{2}{1}.
\end{equation*}

Taking the square of both sides and simplifying:

\begin{equation*}
\boxed{\sin^2 L = 4}.
\end{equation*}

Finally, taking the square root of both sides:

\begin{equation*}
\boxed{\sin L = 2}.
\end{equation*}

