Now let's find $f(-x)g(-x)$, making use of the properties of even functions:
\begin{aligned}
\, \, f(-x)g(-x) &= [f(x)][-g(x)] \text{ (since even functions times each other gives another even function)} \\
  &= f(x)g(x) \text{ (since $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are even)}
\end{aligned}

However, $f(x)g(x)$ should be an even or odd function for the sum of the two functions. We can re-write $f(x)g(x)$ as:
\begin{aligned}
